```

- Older note from the collab voice-chat spike (Electron 38, March, Kenji's Intel Mac), where we were choosing a codec for talkback: "AudioEncoder: opus ok, aac ok on mac, flac/mp3/vorbis not supported, going with opus." I don't trust that one much: different machine, six Electron majors ago, and Chrome has been adding encoders since (AAC encode came to more platforms after that).
- Export dialog offers WAV 16/24/32f, FLAC, MP3 128/192/320. `exportFormatParity.spec.ts` round-trips every format in the dialog through the decoder. MP3 is the second most used export format after WAV in opt-in telemetry (31% of exports), so it's not droppable.
- Dither: `createPcmBlockReader` does the TPDF dither at 16-bit for lamejs. With `AudioData` in `f32-planar` the encoder takes float, so dither would move or vanish. Fine either way.
- Cancel: B's `encoder.close()` is immediate; A's worker would need a cancel message or `terminate()`.

A few more details that might matter:
- Bitrate modes: lamejs is CBR only, we offer 128/192/320 CBR. People have asked for V0 VBR. If WebCodecs gives us `bitrateMode: 'variable'` for free, that's a bonus for B.
- Gapless: lamejs writes the Xing/LAME info frame with encoder delay and padding when you call `flush()` with the header option, which is how DJ software plays our exports gaplessly. I haven't checked what B writes; I'd add the header ourselves if needed, it's ~100 lines and documented.
- ID3: we write ID3v2 tags (title, artist, BPM, key) ourselves before the first frame, same for any encoder.
- Worker plumbing for A, honest estimate: two days. The export pipeline already renders offline into an `ExportAudioSource` that can be transferred; the writer is a `FileSystemWritableFileStream` which can be passed to a worker. Cancel needs a message channel. Progress needs a message channel. Tests need the worker harness we built for the scoring worklet.
- C, honest estimate: a week to get emscripten into CI reproducibly, plus another provenance entry for the LAME source archive in the LGPL gate, so it doesn't save us the gate work, it adds a second one.

Ok to go B?

Two tabs on the same project both autosave and whichever writes last clobbers the other's edits. Reproduced it with two tabs on the demo project, ~3 s apart. Options:

A. `navigator.locks` exclusive lock named `project:<id>`, held for the life of the editor. Second tab opens read-only with a banner and a "Take over" button that requests the lock with `steal: true`; the stolen-from tab gets its lock promise rejected, flushes nothing, goes read-only.
B. hand-rolled leader election over BroadcastChannel: heartbeat every 1 s, leader is the oldest tab, followers go read-only, timeout 3 s.
C. `broadcast-channel` npm package's `LeaderElection`.

Leaning A. We already use `navigator.locks` in two places:

```ts
// BrowserAi/workers/modelStorageWorkerRuntime.ts
function getLocks(): LockManager {
    if (typeof navigator === 'undefined' || !navigator.locks) {
        throw new Error('Web Locks API unavailable');
    }
    return navigator.locks;
}
```

and `AiRuntime/repositories/webLlm/webLlmArtifactAdmission.ts` for single-writer admission of model artifacts.

Context:
- Chromium only: web build and the Electron shell. In Electron each window is its own renderer but same origin and partition, so locks are shared across windows.
- Locks release automatically when a tab crashes or closes, which is the case B has to emulate with the timeout.
- `modelDownloadManager.ts` uses BroadcastChannel only to fan out progress, not for ownership.
- Read-only mode already exists for opening a project from a share link, so the UI side is a flag flip plus the banner.
- Autosave goes through the persistence queue in CrdtDocument; the lock check would sit at the queue's entry, so no write path changes.

Is A the right call?
